AI ECONOMIC SCENARIOS  |  US 2030
SOURCE: Anthropic Institute + FT interview (Jack Clark / John Burn-Murdoch)
PURPOSE: boundary conditions, not forecasts

FUNDAMENTAL
  Economy = tasks
  Task states: unchanged | augmented | automated | new tasks created
  Split: knowledge workers vs other workers
  Growth above ~5% requires entirely new industries
  Employment effects can lag capability (not fully visible yet)

SCENARIO TYPES
  BASELINE / NO AI
  MODEST        ~ internet-scale
  SUBSTANTIAL   ~ half of knowledge work doable by AI
  EXTREME       ~ most knowledge work autonomous; near-RSI diffusion

KEY VARIABLES (illustrative, 2025 $)
  GDP 2030
    Modest      +1.6%     ~ $34.1T
    Substantial +8.3%     ~ $36.3T
    Extreme     +32.4%    ~ $44.4T
                (annual growth can hit ~15%; economy doubles ~4.5 yrs)

  KNOWLEDGE WORKERS
    Modest      small / hard-to-see labor impact
    Substantial wages roughly flat; limited job loss
    Extreme     wages down >10%; unemployment ~18% (double-digit)

  OTHER WORKERS
    tend to gain wages / jobs as capital + output expand

  UNEMPLOYMENT (extreme)
    knowledge-worker spike beyond typical recession
    overall unemployment can sit well above normal
    demand question: who buys output if many are jobless?
    answer in model: capital share rises; new industries needed

WHAT THE MODEL IS / IS NOT
  IS    parameter sweep over capability, adoption, new-task creation
  IS    tool to compare worldviews (normal tech vs singularity-nigh)
  IS NOT a single prediction
  Clark personal lean (not Anthropic official): between Substantial and Extreme

DISCUSSION HOOKS
  delayed visibility of employment shift
  headlines of 15% GDP + soaring unemployment can feed anti-AI sentiment
  space data centers / complementary research cited in the chat
  usefulness of many scenarios vs "we have no clue"

SHARE NOTE
  Scenarios explore extremes so institutions can plan.
  High growth without new industries and demand is internally fragile.
